  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 306 AM EST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nNow through Tuesday...\n\nCurrent sat/obs show cloudy skies across the region with \ntemperatures ranging from the upper 50s to the lower 60s. Antecedent \ndry atmosphere will continue to take some time to moisten from top \nto bottom.  Saturation has already occurred out across far western \nKentucky where a few light rain showers were observed.  For the \nremainder of the overnight period, we'll see cloudy skies continue \nwith scattered sprinkles mainly west of the I-65 corridor.  As a \nweak LLJ starts to develop, combination of isentropic lift and \nsynoptic scale lift from an approaching shortwave trough axis should \nresult in an increase in rain showers, in the I-65 corridor and \npoints west.\n\nFor today, aforementioned shortwave trough axis will lift \nnortheastward across the region.  A weak LLJ will gradually \ntranslate eastward through the day.  Low-level dry air will likely \nhold on during the morning and will limit overall areal coverage of \nrain showers.  However, top-down moistening will occur and light \nrain showers are expected across the region this afternoon and into \nthis evening before the shortwave trough pulls northeast of the \nregion.  Rainfall totals will likely be in some sort of gradient \nwith a tenth of an inch or less across southern Indiana and into the \nBluegrass region of central Kentucky.  Rainfall amounts of a quarter \nto perhaps a half inch will be possible across our southwest areas. \nClouds will keep daytime highs a bit cooler today with lower 60s \nexpected across the southwest areas, elsewhere highs in the mid-\nupper 60s are expected.  Lows tonight will cool back into the upper \n40s/lower 50s.\n\nFor Monday/Tuesday, short duration ridging will build across the \narea ahead of a deepening trough axis in the Plains.  A warm \nsouthwest flow will be seen across the region with highs on Monday \nwarming into the upper 60s/lower 70s over southern IN and into the \nBluegrass region.  Lower 70s are expected in the I-65 corridor and \ndown into the Bowling Green area.  Lows Monday night will be in the \nlower 50s in the I-75 corridor and in the mid-upper 50s elsewhere. \nDry/milder/breezy conditions are expected on Tuesday with highs \nwarming into the upper 70s.  Would not be surprised to see a few low \n80s in spots given the strong warm advective scheme that will be in \nplace.\n\nTuesday Night through Saturday...\n\nBy Tuesday night, a large closed upper level low is forecast to be \nover the upper Midwest with a large trough axis extending southward \nalong the MS River.  A north-south oriented cold front will move \ninto western KY late Tuesday evening and will cross through central \nKY Wednesday morning.  Ahead of the front a line of showers and \nperhaps a few embedded thunderstorms.  The main trend in tonight's \nguidance is a faster progression of the front through the region on \nWednesday and much drier forecast for Thursday.  By Friday a \nsecondary upper level wave will move through the region bringing \nanother round of rain showers to the region.  A third upper level \nwave looks to enter the region by Sunday and will bring another \nround of showers to the area.  Total rainfall with these systems \nwill be on the order of 1-2 inches with some localized 3+ inch \namounts.  Highs Wed/Thu will be in the upper 60s to around 70 with \novernight lows in the 50s. Cooler temps are expected on Fri/Sat with \nreadings in the mid-upper 60s.",
